::ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A population starts at 2000 and grows 3% per year. Which model fits? {
= P(t)=2000(1.03)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial amount is 2000 and 3% growth gives factor 1.03.
~ P(t)=2000(0.97)<sup>t</sup>#This models 3% decay.
~ P(t)=1.03(2000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses initial amount and factor.
~ P(t)=2000+3t#This is linear growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A substance starts at 120 grams and keeps 80% each hour. Which model fits? {
~ A(t)=120(1.20)<sup>t</sup>#This models 20% growth.
= A(t)=120(0.80)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Keeping 80% each hour means the repeated factor is 0.80.
~ A(t)=0.80(120)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses initial amount and factor.
~ A(t)=120-80t#This subtracts a fixed amount and can become negative quickly.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 500(1.04)<sup>t</sup>=750 for t. {
~ t = log(1.04)/log(750/500)#This reverses the change-of-base ratio.
~ t = 750/(500)(1.04)#This does not isolate the exponent.
= t = log(750/500)/log(1.04)#Correct. Divide by 500, then use logarithms to solve for the exponent.
~ t = 750 - 500 - 1.04#This treats the model as linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>In V(t)=30000(0.88)<sup>t</sup>, what does 0.88 mean? {
~ 88% is lost each year.#If 88% were lost, the factor would be 0.12.
~ The starting value is 0.88.#The starting value is 30000.
~ The model grows by 88%.#A factor less than 1 indicates decay.
= 88% of the value remains each year.#Correct. A factor of 0.88 means the value keeps 88% and loses 12% per year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why are logarithms useful in exponential application problems? {
= They solve for an unknown exponent or time.#Correct. Logarithms undo exponential expressions, so they help find time.
~ They always find the initial value.#Initial values usually come from context or the coefficient.
~ They turn every model linear.#They solve equations but do not change the model type.
~ They remove the need for units.#Application answers still need units and interpretation.
}

